WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.947
One client, many backends: OpenAI-compatible endpoints.

2
00:00:04.227 --> 00:00:12.611
Ollama, llama-server, LM Studio, and mlx-lm’s server all expose an OpenAI-style chat completions endpoint.

3
00:00:12.891 --> 00:00:30.683
In practice that means you can point the official openai Python client at http://localhost:11434/v1 (Ollama’s docs pass a placeholder API key, which it ignores) and reuse code written for a hosted API.

4
00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:36.936
That is useful for swapping backends in an eval harness, and for tools that only speak that protocol.

5
00:00:37.216 --> 00:00:41.397
“Compatible” is a promise about the shape of requests, not about behavior.

6
00:00:41.677 --> 00:00:46.157
Ollama’s docs say it supports a subset of the OpenAI API.

7
00:00:46.437 --> 00:00:55.888
Model names differ per server, some parameters are ignored, structured output support varies, and usage numbers come from each server’s own tokenizer.

8
00:00:56.168 --> 00:01:15.091
So verify three things on any local endpoint: which model actually answered (list /v1/models, or ollama ps ), which quantization it is running (in the model tag or GGUF name), and that a field your code depends on is really honored, by testing it rather than reading about it.
